# sumBASIC 0.1.0a2

sumBASIC is an educational BASIC frontend for the Sum ecosystem. This alpha focuses on text-mode programs and now includes classic BASIC file channels, fixed-record random access, standard streams/pipelines, and an initial bridge to the xBase-compatible database engine used by sumX.

The graphical `SCREEN`/drawing family remains planned for the shared Sum Machine video subsystem rather than being implemented separately in BASIC.

## Implemented

- classic numbered program entry and unnumbered structured source;
- immediate mode with `RUN`, `LIST`, `NEW`, `LOAD`, `SAVE`, `DELETE`, `RENUM`;
- `PRINT` / `?`, `INPUT`, `LINE INPUT`, `CLS`, `LOCATE`;
- exponentiation with BASIC `^` (`? 5^3` prints `125`);
- assignments and BASIC suffixes `$`, `%`, `&`, `!`, `#`;
- `IF ... THEN ... ELSE`, block `IF ... END IF`;
- `FOR ... TO ... STEP ... NEXT`, `WHILE/WEND`, `DO/LOOP`;
- `GOTO`, `GOSUB`, `RETURN`, `END`, `STOP`;
- `DATA`, `READ`, `RESTORE`, `SWAP`, initial `DIM`;
- common numeric and string functions;
- sequential, binary and random-access file channels;
- `FIELD`, `GET`, `PUT`, `INPUT #`, `LINE INPUT #`, `PRINT #`, `WRITE #`;
- `FREEFILE()`, `EOF()`, `LOF()`, `LOC()`;
- named `STDIN`, `STDOUT`, `STDERR` streams and shell-pipeline endpoints;
- channels `1..10` with xBase-style aliases `A..J`;
- initial `DB` bridge backed by the sumX xBase-compatible work-area/database engine;
- sumTUI command-window frontend and `sumedit` integration.

Not yet implemented: `SUB/FUNCTION`, `SELECT CASE`, complete arrays, events/errors, SumIR emission, Python/native compilation, graphics, virtual `PEEK/POKE` and port I/O.

## File channels

Classic sequential access:

```basic
OPEN "people.txt" FOR OUTPUT AS #1
WRITE #1, "Ada", 37
CLOSE #1

OPEN "people.txt" FOR INPUT AS #A
INPUT #A, name$, age%
CLOSE #A
PRINT name$; " "; age%
```

`A..J` are aliases for channels `1..10`, matching the work-area numbering convention used by xBase `SELECT`.

The fopen-like form is also accepted:

```basic
OPEN "notes.txt" MODE "r" AS #1
OPEN "output.txt" MODE "w" AS #2
OPEN "events.log" MODE "a" AS #3
OPEN "data.bin" MODE "r+b" AS #4
```

Supported mode strings include `r`, `w`, `a`, `r+`, `w+`, `a+` and their binary `b` forms.

### Random records and FIELD

```basic
OPEN "people.dat" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN=40
FIELD #1, 24 AS name$, 8 AS born$, 8 AS height$

name$ = "John O'Connor"
born$ = "19850228"
height$ = "1.82"
PUT #1, 1

name$ = "": born$ = "": height$ = ""
GET #1, 1
PRINT name$; " "; born$; " "; height$
CLOSE #1
```

`FIELD` variables are fixed-width string fields in this first implementation, compatible with the classic BASIC record-buffer model.

## Standard streams and pipelines

Standard streams can be attached to ordinary BASIC channels:

```basic
OPEN STDIN FOR INPUT AS #1
LINE INPUT #1, line$
CLOSE #1

OPEN STDOUT FOR OUTPUT AS #2
PRINT #2, line$
CLOSE #2

OPEN STDERR FOR OUTPUT AS #3
PRINT #3, "diagnostic"
CLOSE #3
```

A source beginning or ending with `|` represents a pipeline. Input example:

```basic
OPEN "| printf 'Ada\n'" FOR INPUT AS #1
LINE INPUT #1, name$
CLOSE #1
PRINT name$
```

Output pipelines use `FOR OUTPUT` in the same way. Pipeline commands execute in the host shell and therefore should be treated like any other explicit shell command supplied by the program author.

## xBase database bridge

The interpreter exposes a `BasicDatabase` object backed by the sumX database/work-area implementation. BASIC-side statements currently include:

```basic
DB.SELECT A
DB.USE "students"
DB.GO TOP
DB.SKIP 1
PRINT DB.RECNO(); "/"; DB.RECCOUNT()
DB.CLOSE
```

The bridge intentionally shares the same work-area idea as sumX so database-oriented BASIC programs and later Sum frontends can converge on one runtime rather than duplicating database semantics.

## Usage

```bash
sumbasic
sumbasic --plain
sumbasic program.bas
sumbasic --run program.bas
sumbasic --check program.bas
```

Classic immediate mode accepts numbered lines as well as direct statements:

```text
> ? 5^3
125
> 10 PRINT "HELLO"
> 20 FOR I%=1 TO 3
> 30 PRINT I%
> 40 NEXT I%
> 50 END
> LIST
> RUN
```

